Subject: Single species, single season detection history using multiple survey periods
Message-ID: <uGypJ0zwQP4.-CXPHbuKAAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/uGypJ0zwQP4/m/-CXPHbuKAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

If I conduct a 14-night survey (14 consecutive days), then a month later conduct another 14 surveys, is it appropriate to combine these two 'surveys periods' into the one detection history (i.e. 28 survey occasions, from two survey periods) assuming the population is closed? Or should 'survey period' be included as a covariate (i.e. with 14 occasions?).

Thanks!

From u00ebeb6e0521112d at groups.google.com  Fri Sep 10 06:53:30 2021
From: u00ebeb6e0521112d at groups.google.com (Marc Kery)
Date: Fri, 10 Sep 2021 06:53:30 +0000
Subject: Re: [unmarked] Single species, single season detection history using multiple survey periods
Message-ID: <uGypJ0zwQP4.OpDxIPSdAAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <uGypJ0zwQP4.-CXPHbuKAAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
References: <uGypJ0zwQP4.-CXPHbuKAAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/uGypJ0zwQP4/m/OpDxIPSdAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Tim,

that's not really a statistical question but one that must be decided upon based on your knowledge of the species and the sampling protocol: if you think that there is little or no change in the true presence and absence states of your surveyed sites over the entire 28 occasion-period, then you can put all the data in one detection history. Then you can still estimate different values for p (e.g. as a trend over the entire 28-d period or as a difference for one period vs. the other), but occupancy can then not change.

If on the other hand you think it's safer to relax the assumption of closure for the full 28 days then you should either choose the dynamic occupancy model and allow for openness between the two 14-d periods by estimation of one colonization and one extinction parameter. Or else "stack" the two 14-day chunks of data and add a single covariate that indexes the period and then fit a closed model, but with separate values of occupancy for each period.

I think I would choose one of the two latter solutions. If these analyses then suggest little or no differences in psi between periods, or little or no dynamics, you could then still revert the analysis back to the simple analysis mentioned above first.

Some might argue that stacking involves a kind of pseudo-replication, since site ID is not recognized. If you use unmarked's "sister package" ubms (available from CRAN) you could stack and add a site random effect to account for any persistent site effects not modelled by your covariates.

Best regards  -- Marc

