> Typical regression models (and in the widest sense the Nmix is a regression model) make no assumptions at all about any distribution of the explanatory values (this is a quite widespread mis-conception). Hence, strictly there is no need at all to transform them. However, it may help the numerical likelihood maximisation routines if you do. Plus, it may make comparison among covariates more straightforward if you standardize them all, e.g., you can then more easily say which one has the "strongest influence"  by looking at the absolute magnitude of their estimates.
> Best regards  -- Marc
